 ## Overview
-Voxel Meshing with Smooth Lighting is a surface extraction algorithm that generates a single quad mesh for exposed voxel faces within a chunk, populating vertex buffers with position, normal, texture coordinates, and dual-lighting colors (sun and block light) [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from flat lighting methods by sampling eight neighboring voxels per face to compute smooth vertex lighting transitions, while simultaneously handling opaque and alpha (transparent) voxels through separate index buffers [S1, unofficial]. This method builds on [Voxel Face Mesh Generation](4212-voxel-face-mesh-generation.md) by adding the complexity of neighbor-based light interpolation and boundary chunk data resolution.
+Voxel Meshing with Smooth Lighting is a surface extraction algorithm that generates a single quad mesh for exposed voxel faces, populating a `MeshData` buffer with vertices, indices, normals, UVs, tangents, and vertex colors based on face direction and block type [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from flat-shaded siblings by sampling 8 neighboring voxels per face to calculate smooth lighting transitions for both sun and block light, effectively baking ambient occlusion and gradient lighting into the vertex colors [S1, S2, unofficial]. This method handles both opaque and alpha (transparent) voxels, separating them into distinct index buffers for correct rendering order [S1, unofficial].
 
